WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.690
The next type of insert statement inserts
values into a table and does not involve using

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.150
a SELECT statement.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:14.320
To execute such an insert statement, we need
to know the table we are inserting data into,

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:21.180
the columns that will receive data, and the
data itself.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.169
Here, I have an INSERT statement that shows
that we will insert some data into the BikeRepairs

00:00:27.169 --> 00:00:28.169
table.

00:00:28.169 --> 00:00:34.800
The statement starts with the INSERT INTO
keywords and the table we are inserting data

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.469
into: BikeRepairs.

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:43.989
Then in parentheses, we see the columns that
will receive data.

00:00:43.989 --> 00:00:47.800
Keep in mind that required fields must be
included here.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:56.800
The VALUES keyword is next, followed by the
values we will add, making sure each value

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:03.690
corresponds with each column listed above.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:08.040
For this example, I am inserting two repairs
into the table.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.479
In real-life apps, code that pulls values
from form fields is a far more likely scenario

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:15.960
than for this example of hard-coded values.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:22.140
Still, the goal is to see how an INSERT statement
is formed and executed.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:28.859
I will execute this statement, and we have
two rows added to the BikeRepairs table.

